Rate of transmission of heat

\begin{array}{l}
=\frac{\text { Temperature difference }}{\text { Thermal Resistance }} \\
\therefore \quad \frac{d Q}{d t}=\frac{d \theta}{R}
\end{array}

Here, \frac{d Q}{d t}=\frac{\left(\theta-\theta_{2}\right)}{R_{2}}=\frac{\theta_{1}-\theta}{R_{1}}
\begin{array}{l}
\Rightarrow \frac{\theta-\theta_{2}}{R_{2}}=\frac{\theta_{1}-\theta}{R_{1}} \\
\Rightarrow \quad R_{1} \theta-R_{1} \theta_{2}=R_{2} \theta_{1}-R_{2} \theta \\
\Rightarrow \quad \theta\left(R_{1}+R_{2}\right)=R_{2} \theta_{1}+R_{1} \theta_{2} \\
\therefore \quad \theta=\frac{\left(R_{2} \theta_{1}+R_{1} \theta_{2}\right)}{\left(R_{1}+R_{2}\right)}
\end{array}

Volume of the metal at $30^{\circ} \mathrm{C}$,

\begin{aligned}
V_{30} &=\frac{\text { loss of weight }}{\text { specific gravity } \times g} \\
&=\frac{(45-25) g}{1.5 \times g}=13.33 \mathrm{~cm}^{3}
\end{aligned}

Similarly, volume of metal at $40^{\circ} \mathrm{C}$
V_{40}=\frac{(45-27) g}{1.25 \times g}=14.40 \mathrm{~cm}^{3}

Now, $V_{40}=V_{30}\left[1+\gamma\left(t_{2}-t_{1}\right)\right]$
\text { or } \begin{aligned}
& \gamma=\frac{V_{40}-V_{30}}{V_{30}\left(t_{2}-t_{1}\right)}=\frac{14.40-13.33}{13.33(40-30)} \\
&=8.03 \times 10^{-3} /{ }^{\circ} \mathrm{C}
\end{aligned}

$\therefore \quad$ Coefficient of linear expansion of the metal

\alpha=\frac{\gamma}{3}=\frac{8.03 \times 10^{-3}}{3} \approx 2.6 \times 10^{-3} /{ }^{\circ} \mathrm{C}

